{"authored" [{"question" "q-97417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=2t-{t}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "09dde97fbd5912e561fd4e68e4e2e87eeb966de476d17333b3611bd502e1f6af" "basis" 1849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2t-{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=1,\\,p(0)=0,\\,p(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(0)p(0)+p(-1)p(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(0)]^{2}+[p(-1)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/state.json" "file_sha256" "09dde97fbd5912e561fd4e68e4e2e87eeb966de476d17333b3611bd502e1f6af" "basis" 1849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-97417" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "80fec36a7cafbb2fd5ad84